{{define "title"}}Share a project plan with people who have no MS Project{{end}}

{{define "main"}}
<main class="page page--text centered">
  <div class="page__head">
    <h1>Share a plan with people who cannot open it</h1>
  </div>

  {{with .Flash}}<p class="flash">{{.}}</p>{{end}}

  <p class="lead">
    You keep the schedule. They keep asking what it says. A link works in their browser - no
    licence, no install, nothing for them to figure out.
  </p>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>The routine this replaces</h2>

    <p>
      If you plan work for a living, one part of the job has nothing to do with planning. The
      schedule updates on Friday, and then you spend twenty minutes making it readable for people
      who do not own Microsoft Project: export to PDF, crop the timeline so it fits, attach it to
      an email, and answer the three replies asking what changed since last week.
    </p>

    <p>
      The PDF is the compromise everyone has settled for, and it is a bad one. It is a photograph
      of a schedule. Nobody can collapse the phases they do not own, change the scale from days to
      quarters to see the shape of the year, click a task to find out what it waits for, or search
      for their own name. Every one of those questions comes back to you as a message, which is
      why the twenty minutes is never really twenty minutes.
    </p>

    <h2>What a link gives them instead</h2>

    <p>
      Open your <code>.mpp</code> here, save it, and share the link. What lands in their browser is
      the plan itself, not a picture of it: the task hierarchy with phases they can fold away,
      dependency arrows, the critical path, non-working days shaded from your project calendar, and
      full detail on any task they click - dates, progress, predecessors, resources, notes.
    </p>

    <p>
      They need no account and install nothing. It works the same on Windows, macOS and Linux,
      which matters more than it sounds: Microsoft Project has no macOS version at all, so the
      person on a Mac has never been able to open your attachments.
    </p>

    <h2>What they cannot do</h2>

    <p>
      Read it. That is the whole list. Nobody can move a bar, change a date, reschedule anything or
      alter the file you hold - this is a viewer, deliberately, and there is no edit mode to find.
      The plan you sent is the plan they see, and the copy on your machine stays the only one that
      can change.
    </p>

    <p>
      Sharing is revocable. Switch a plan back to private and the link stops working the same
      second, for everyone who has it. You can also put a password on a link, so forwarding it does
      not widen the audience by accident.
    </p>

    <h2>What it costs</h2>

    <p>
      Reading is free for them, always, with no account and no quota - that is the point of the
      whole thing. On your side a free account keeps 20 plans and 2 live links at a time, which is
      enough to try it on a real project. Pro removes both limits and raises the upload size to
      50 MB, and during early access Pro costs nothing.
    </p>

    <p class="plans__cta">
      {{if .UserEmail}}
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/">Open a plan to share</a>
      {{else}}
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/signup">Create a free account</a>
      {{end}}
      <a href="/pricing">See the plans</a>
    </p>

    <h2>Common questions</h2>

    <dl class="faq">
      <dt>Does the recipient need an account?</dt>
      <dd>No. They open the link and read the plan. They need an account only if they want to save plans of their own.</dd>

      <dt>Can they change my schedule?</dt>
      <dd>No. There is no editing anywhere in the product, for anyone. The file you uploaded is never modified and cannot be written back to.</dd>

      <dt>What if I send the wrong plan?</dt>
      <dd>Make it private again and the link dies immediately, including for people who already have it. Access is checked on every request, not baked into the link.</dd>

      <dt>Can I stop the link spreading?</dt>
      <dd>Put a password on it. The link then asks for the password before it shows anything, so forwarding it alone is not enough.</dd>

      <dt>Will they see the same dates I see?</dt>
      <dd>Yes. Everything is read out of your file rather than recalculated - the same hierarchy, dates, durations, dependencies, critical tasks and calendar. Only the styling is ours.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>
</main>

{{template "sitefooter" .}}
{{end}}
